    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessService" name="eInvoicing" id="id-040f1c5d80b94b188b94d1fbb94c602a">
      <documentation>Definition: eInvoicing enables public organisations to issue, transmit and deliver electronic invoice documents in a structured data format to its providers/suppliers, according to European standards.

Source: European Commission

Source reference: https://ec.europa.eu/digital-building-blocks/wikis/display/DIGITAL/What+is+eInvoicing

Additional information: eInvoicing, within the context of IT interoperability, refers to the electronic exchange of invoices between businesses, governments, and other entities in a standardized and structured format. It is a digital public service provided by the European Commission to facilitate seamless and efficient invoicing processes across the European Union.

eInvoicing eliminates the need for traditional paper-based invoices, enabling organizations to send, receive, process, and store invoices electronically. It aims to streamline and automate the invoicing process, reducing administrative burdens, enhancing accuracy, and improving overall efficiency.

In the European Commission's digital public service, eInvoicing adheres to a set of standards and technical specifications known as the European eInvoicing Standard (EN). This standard ensures that invoices are exchanged in a consistent and interoperable manner, regardless of the systems or software used by the sender and receiver.

The eInvoicing process begins with the creation of an electronic invoice by the supplier. The invoice is then transmitted to the recipient through a secure electronic channel, such as a dedicated eInvoicing platform or a government-provided portal. The recipient's system receives the invoice and automatically processes it, extracting relevant data and integrating it into their financial systems.

To ensure seamless interoperability, the European eInvoicing Standard defines a common syntax and structure for invoices, including mandatory and optional data fields. This standardization enables different systems to understand and interpret invoices consistently, regardless of their origin or destination. It also facilitates the automation of invoice processing, as systems can easily extract and validate data without manual intervention.

eInvoicing within the European Commission's digital public service also incorporates other features to enhance efficiency and security. These may include digital signatures or electronic seals to ensure the authenticity and integrity of invoices, as well as encryption techniques to protect sensitive information during transmission.

By promoting eInvoicing, the European Commission aims to foster a digital single market, where businesses can seamlessly trade across borders without unnecessary barriers. It encourages the adoption of eInvoicing by both public and private entities, offering guidance, support, and technical resources to facilitate the transition from traditional paper-based invoicing to electronic processes.

Overall, eInvoicing within the context of IT interoperability is a digital public service provided by the European Commission to enable the electronic exchange of standardized invoices. It aims to streamline invoicing processes, reduce administrative burdens, and enhance efficiency, ultimately contributing to the creation of a digital single market within the European Union.

Example: One example of the application of eInvoicing within the context of IT interoperability in a digital public service at the European Commission could be the implementation of a centralized eInvoicing platform. This platform would enable the electronic exchange of invoices between the European Commission and its suppliers, ensuring seamless interoperability between different IT systems.

Through this platform, suppliers could submit their invoices electronically in a standardized format, such as the European standard for eInvoicing (EN16931). The platform would then automatically validate and process these invoices, ensuring compliance with relevant regulations and standards.

Furthermore, the eInvoicing platform could integrate with existing financial management systems used by the European Commission, allowing for the direct transfer of invoice data into these systems. This integration would eliminate the need for manual data entry, reducing errors and improving efficiency.

Additionally, the eInvoicing platform could support various authentication and authorization mechanisms, such as digital signatures or electronic identification, to ensure the security and authenticity of the invoicing process. This would enable secure electronic transmission of invoices, eliminating the need for paper-based invoices and manual handling.

Moreover, the platform could provide functionalities for tracking and monitoring the status of invoices, allowing both the European Commission and its suppliers to have real-time visibility into the invoicing process. This would facilitate better communication and collaboration between the parties involved, reducing delays and improving overall transparency.

Overall, the implementation of a centralized eInvoicing platform within the European Commission's digital public service would enhance IT interoperability by enabling seamless electronic exchange of invoices, integration with existing systems, and ensuring compliance with standards and regulations.

LOST view: TVA-API Enablers [Motivation]

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EInvoicingBusinessService

EIRA traceability: eira:DigitalPublicBusinessService

ABB name: egovera:EInvoicingBusinessService

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-04

dct:identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EInvoicingBusinessService

dct:title: eInvoicing Digital Public Service

</documentation>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EInvoicingBusinessService"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EInvoicingBusinessService"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="eInvoicing Digital Public Service"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:EInvoicingBusinessService"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="European Commission"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference" value="https://ec.europa.eu/digital-building-blocks/wikis/display/DIGITAL/What+is+eInvoicing"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="One example of the application of eInvoicing within the context of IT interoperability in a digital public service at the European Commission could be the implementation of a centralized eInvoicing platform. This platform would enable the electronic exchange of invoices between the European Commission and its suppliers, ensuring seamless interoperability between different IT systems.&#xA;&#xA;Through this platform, suppliers could submit their invoices electronically in a standardized format, such as the European standard for eInvoicing (EN16931). The platform would then automatically validate and process these invoices, ensuring compliance with relevant regulations and standards.&#xA;&#xA;Furthermore, the eInvoicing platform could integrate with existing financial management systems used by the European Commission, allowing for the direct transfer of invoice data into these systems. This integration would eliminate the need for manual data entry, reducing errors and improving efficiency.&#xA;&#xA;Additionally, the eInvoicing platform could support various authentication and authorization mechanisms, such as digital signatures or electronic identification, to ensure the security and authenticity of the invoicing process. This would enable secure electronic transmission of invoices, eliminating the need for paper-based invoices and manual handling.&#xA;&#xA;Moreover, the platform could provide functionalities for tracking and monitoring the status of invoices, allowing both the European Commission and its suppliers to have real-time visibility into the invoicing process. This would facilitate better communication and collaboration between the parties involved, reducing delays and improving overall transparency.&#xA;&#xA;Overall, the implementation of a centralized eInvoicing platform within the European Commission's digital public service would enhance IT interoperability by enabling seamless electronic exchange of invoices, integration with existing systems, and ensuring compliance with standards and regulations."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="eInvoicing, within the context of IT interoperability, refers to the electronic exchange of invoices between businesses, governments, and other entities in a standardized and structured format. It is a digital public service provided by the European Commission to facilitate seamless and efficient invoicing processes across the European Union.&#xA;&#xA;eInvoicing eliminates the need for traditional paper-based invoices, enabling organizations to send, receive, process, and store invoices electronically. It aims to streamline and automate the invoicing process, reducing administrative burdens, enhancing accuracy, and improving overall efficiency.&#xA;&#xA;In the European Commission's digital public service, eInvoicing adheres to a set of standards and technical specifications known as the European eInvoicing Standard (EN). This standard ensures that invoices are exchanged in a consistent and interoperable manner, regardless of the systems or software used by the sender and receiver.&#xA;&#xA;The eInvoicing process begins with the creation of an electronic invoice by the supplier. The invoice is then transmitted to the recipient through a secure electronic channel, such as a dedicated eInvoicing platform or a government-provided portal. The recipient's system receives the invoice and automatically processes it, extracting relevant data and integrating it into their financial systems.&#xA;&#xA;To ensure seamless interoperability, the European eInvoicing Standard defines a common syntax and structure for invoices, including mandatory and optional data fields. This standardization enables different systems to understand and interpret invoices consistently, regardless of their origin or destination. It also facilitates the automation of invoice processing, as systems can easily extract and validate data without manual intervention.&#xA;&#xA;eInvoicing within the European Commission's digital public service also incorporates other features to enhance efficiency and security. These may include digital signatures or electronic seals to ensure the authenticity and integrity of invoices, as well as encryption techniques to protect sensitive information during transmission.&#xA;&#xA;By promoting eInvoicing, the European Commission aims to foster a digital single market, where businesses can seamlessly trade across borders without unnecessary barriers. It encourages the adoption of eInvoicing by both public and private entities, offering guidance, support, and technical resources to facilitate the transition from traditional paper-based invoicing to electronic processes.&#xA;&#xA;Overall, eInvoicing within the context of IT interoperability is a digital public service provided by the European Commission to enable the electronic exchange of standardized invoices. It aims to streamline invoicing processes, reduce administrative burdens, and enhance efficiency, ultimately contributing to the creation of a digital single market within the European Union."/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="eInvoicing enables public organisations to issue, transmit and deliver electronic invoice documents in a structured data format to its providers/suppliers, according to European standards."/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVA-API Enablers [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Functional Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Organisational view [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Organisational view"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Organisational"/>
      <property key="skos:broader" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/DigitalPublicBusinessService"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessService" name="Forms Management" id="id-069ce8640f224c7f86791aaf6905231a">
      <documentation>Definition: Forms Management enables the storage, catalogation and indexing/searching of structured and unstructured documents.

Source: FORTRA

Source reference: https://www.helpsystems.com/blog/what-forms-management

Additional information: Forms management within the context of IT interoperability in a digital public service within the European Commission refers to the systematic and efficient handling of electronic forms used for various administrative processes. It involves the design, creation, distribution, submission, processing, and storage of digital forms to facilitate seamless information exchange and collaboration between different systems, organizations, and individuals.

At its core, forms management aims to streamline and standardize the collection of data and information by providing a structured and user-friendly interface for users to input and submit relevant details electronically. This process eliminates the need for paper-based forms, reducing administrative burdens, improving data accuracy, and enhancing overall efficiency.

The European Commission, being a supranational institution responsible for policy-making and governance within the European Union, relies heavily on digital public services to interact with citizens, businesses, and other stakeholders. Forms management plays a crucial role in ensuring the smooth functioning of these services by enabling the exchange of information in a secure, standardized, and interoperable manner.

The process of forms management typically involves several key components. Firstly, there is the design and creation of electronic forms, which involves defining the required fields, data types, validation rules, and any associated business logic. These forms are often developed using specialized software tools that allow for easy customization and integration with existing systems.

Once the forms are created, they need to be distributed to the relevant users or organizations. This can be achieved through various channels, such as web portals, email notifications, or integration with other systems. The forms may also be made available in multiple languages to cater to the diverse linguistic needs of the European Union.

When users fill out the forms, the data they provide is submitted electronically to the designated recipients or back-end systems. This data may undergo validation checks to ensure its accuracy and compliance with predefined rules. Forms management systems often incorporate features like data encryption and digital signatures to ensure the security and integrity of the information being transmitted.

After submission, the received forms are processed and stored in a centralized repository or database. This allows for easy retrieval and access to the collected data for further processing or analysis. The forms management system may also generate notifications or trigger automated workflows based on predefined rules, ensuring timely actions and responses.

In the context of IT interoperability, forms management systems need to adhere to established standards and protocols to enable seamless integration with other systems and services. This includes using open data formats, standardized data models, and interoperability frameworks. By adopting such standards, the European Commission can ensure that its digital public services can effectively communicate and exchange information with other systems, both within and outside the organization.

Overall, forms management within the European Commission's digital public service is a critical component of IT interoperability. It enables the efficient collection, processing, and storage of electronic forms, facilitating seamless information exchange and collaboration between different stakeholders. By leveraging digital technologies and adhering to interoperability standards, the European Commission can enhance the effectiveness and accessibility of its public services, ultimately benefiting citizens, businesses, and the overall European Union.

Example: Forms management is an essential aspect of IT interoperability in the context of a digital public service in the European Commission. Here are some examples of its application:

1. Online Application Forms: The European Commission can utilize forms management to create and manage online application forms for various services such as grants, permits, or registrations. These forms can be designed to collect specific information required from applicants, ensuring consistency and standardization across different services.

2. Data Collection and Reporting: Forms management can be employed to collect data from various stakeholders, such as citizens, businesses, or other government agencies. For instance, the European Commission may use forms to gather information on the implementation of specific policies or to collect feedback from citizens on public services. This data can then be analyzed and reported on to support evidence-based decision-making.

3. Service Requests and Complaints: Forms management can facilitate the submission and processing of service requests or complaints. Citizens can fill out online forms to report issues, request information, or seek assistance. These forms can be integrated with backend systems to ensure efficient handling and tracking of requests, leading to improved service delivery and responsiveness.

4. Cross-Border Collaboration: In the context of the European Commission, forms management can support cross-border collaboration between member states. For instance, forms can be used to exchange information and data between different national authorities, enabling seamless cooperation and interoperability in areas such as law enforcement, public health, or environmental protection.

5. Standardization and Harmonization: Forms management can contribute to standardizing and harmonizing data collection processes across different services and member states. By defining common data fields, formats, and validation rules, forms can ensure consistency in the information collected, making it easier to aggregate and analyze data at a European level.

6. Accessibility and Multilingual Support: Forms management can address accessibility requirements by providing accessible form templates and features such as screen reader compatibility or alternative text for visual elements. Additionally, multilingual support can be incorporated into forms, allowing citizens to complete them in their preferred language, thereby enhancing inclusivity and user experience.

Overall, forms management plays a crucial role in enabling efficient data collection, standardization, and collaboration within the European Commission's digital public services, supporting IT interoperability and enhancing the delivery of services to citizens and stakeholders.

LOST view: TVA-Data Management Enablers [Motivation]

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/FormsManagementBusinessService

EIRA traceability: eira:DigitalPublicBusinessService

ABB name: egovera:FormsManagementBusinessService

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-04

