</documentation>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/OpenDataManagementBusinessService"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/OpenDataManagementBusinessService"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Open Data Management Digital Public Service"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:OpenDataManagementBusinessService"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="Open Data Handbook"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference" value="https://opendatahandbook.org/"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="One example of the application of Open Data Management within the context of IT interoperability in a digital public service at the European Commission is the provision of standardized and open APIs (Application Programming Interfaces) for accessing and exchanging data.&#xA;&#xA;By adopting open standards and providing APIs, the European Commission enables interoperability between different IT systems and applications. This allows for seamless data exchange and integration between various digital public services, both within the Commission and with external stakeholders.&#xA;&#xA;For instance, the European Commission could develop and publish open APIs that provide access to datasets related to environmental data, such as air quality measurements or climate change indicators. These APIs would follow open standards, ensuring compatibility and ease of integration with other systems.&#xA;&#xA;By making these APIs openly available, developers from both within the Commission and external organizations can leverage the data to build innovative applications, services, or visualizations. This promotes collaboration, transparency, and the reuse of data across different digital public services.&#xA;&#xA;Furthermore, the European Commission can establish a centralized Open Data Portal that serves as a comprehensive repository of open datasets. This portal would provide a single point of access to a wide range of data, ensuring consistency and ease of discovery for developers and users.&#xA;&#xA;Through effective Open Data Management, the European Commission can foster IT interoperability by promoting the use of open standards, APIs, and centralized data repositories. This approach enables seamless data exchange, collaboration, and innovation across various digital public services, ultimately benefiting citizens, businesses, and policymakers."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="Open Data Management within the context of IT interoperability refers to the process of collecting, organizing, and sharing digital information in a standardized and accessible manner, with the goal of promoting transparency, collaboration, and innovation. In the European Commission, Open Data Management is a crucial aspect of providing digital public services to citizens.&#xA;&#xA;At its core, Open Data Management involves the identification, collection, and publication of various types of data generated or held by public institutions. This data can include statistical information, research findings, reports, legislative texts, geographic data, and much more. The European Commission recognizes the value of this data and aims to make it freely available to the public, businesses, and other organizations.&#xA;&#xA;The process of Open Data Management begins with the identification of relevant datasets within the European Commission's various departments and agencies. These datasets are then assessed for their potential value and relevance to the public. Once identified, the data is collected, ensuring that it adheres to certain quality standards, such as accuracy, completeness, and timeliness.&#xA;&#xA;To ensure interoperability, the European Commission follows established standards and guidelines for data formats, metadata, and data sharing protocols. This ensures that the data can be easily understood, accessed, and used by both humans and machines. Commonly used standards include the Resource Description Framework (RDF), the eXtensible Markup Language (XML), and the Web Ontology Language (OWL).&#xA;&#xA;Once the data is collected and prepared, it is published on dedicated platforms or portals, such as the European Data Portal, which serves as a central hub for accessing and discovering open datasets from various European Union institutions. These platforms provide users with search functionalities, allowing them to find and access the desired datasets based on specific criteria.&#xA;&#xA;Open Data Management also involves ensuring the long-term sustainability and usability of the published datasets. This includes establishing proper data governance frameworks, data lifecycle management, and data preservation strategies. The European Commission aims to keep the datasets up to date, ensuring that any changes or updates are reflected in a timely manner.&#xA;&#xA;Furthermore, Open Data Management within the European Commission emphasizes the importance of data privacy and security. Personal data is handled in accordance with the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and other relevant legislation, ensuring that individuals' privacy rights are respected.&#xA;&#xA;By adopting Open Data Management practices, the European Commission aims to foster a culture of openness, collaboration, and innovation. Open datasets can be utilized by citizens, businesses, researchers, and developers to create new applications, services, and insights. This promotes economic growth, evidence-based policymaking, and citizen engagement.&#xA;&#xA;In summary, Open Data Management within the context of IT interoperability in the European Commission involves the identification, collection, quality assurance, publication, and long-term management of open datasets. It follows established standards and guidelines to ensure interoperability and accessibility. By making data freely available, the European Commission aims to promote transparency, collaboration, and innovation in the provision of digital public services."/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Open Data Management refers to the processes and practices involved in collecting, organizing, publishing, and maintaining open data. It involves the identification, selection, and preparation of datasets for public release, ensuring their quality, usability, and compliance with relevant open data standards and policies. Open Data Management aims to enhance transparency, promote data sharing and reuse, and enable the development of innovative applications and services by providing access to structured, machine-readable data that is freely available to the public."/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVA-Data Management Enablers [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Functional Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Organisational view [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Data Spaces"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Organisational view"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Organisational"/>
      <property key="skos:broader" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/DigitalPublicBusinessService"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessService" name="Waiting list Management" id="id-653e5e712ff4455e81e6f1ad4274014d">
      <documentation>Definition: Waiting List Management enables the management and optimisation of a waiting list / queue of users that are willing to access to a specific public service. This service enables an effective management of available resources and an effective delivery of public services to their users.

Source: John Andrew Bowers

Source reference: enables the management and optimisation of a waiting list / queue of users that are willing to access to a specific public service. This service enables an effective management of available resources and an effective delivery of public services to their users.

Additional information: Waiting list management within the context of IT interoperability in a digital public service in the European Commission refers to the systematic and efficient handling of waiting lists for various services or resources. It involves the use of digital technologies and standardized protocols to ensure seamless communication and coordination between different systems and stakeholders involved in managing waiting lists.

The primary objective of waiting list management is to optimize the allocation and utilization of resources, such as appointments, services, or facilities, while ensuring fairness, transparency, and timely access for individuals or entities on the waiting list. It aims to streamline the process of managing waiting lists, reduce administrative burden, and enhance the overall user experience.

In this digital public service, waiting list management leverages IT interoperability to enable seamless integration and data exchange between various systems, databases, and applications. It ensures that relevant information is shared securely and efficiently among different stakeholders, such as service providers, administrators, and individuals on the waiting list.

The process begins with the registration of individuals or entities on the waiting list, which may involve capturing their personal details, preferences, and specific requirements. This information is securely stored in a centralized database or repository, accessible to authorized personnel involved in managing the waiting list.

As resources become available, the waiting list management system automatically identifies and matches suitable candidates based on predefined criteria, such as priority, urgency, or specific needs. It generates notifications or alerts to inform individuals about their eligibility or availability of the desired service or resource.

The system also facilitates communication and interaction between the service providers and individuals on the waiting list. It may enable online appointment scheduling, real-time updates, and personalized notifications to ensure individuals are informed about their position on the waiting list, estimated waiting times, or any changes in their status.

Moreover, waiting list management within the context of IT interoperability ensures interoperability between different systems and platforms used by various stakeholders. It enables seamless data exchange, synchronization, and integration of information across different databases or applications, eliminating the need for manual data entry or duplication.

Additionally, the system may incorporate advanced analytics and reporting capabilities to generate insights and performance indicators related to waiting times, resource allocation, and overall efficiency. This allows administrators to monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of the waiting list management process, identify bottlenecks or areas for improvement, and make data-driven decisions.

Overall, waiting list management in the European Commission's digital public service leverages IT interoperability to streamline and optimize the allocation of resources, enhance transparency and fairness, and improve the overall user experience. It ensures efficient coordination, communication, and data exchange between stakeholders, ultimately contributing to the delivery of high-quality public services to individuals and entities on the waiting list.

Example: In the context of IT interoperability within a digital public service in the European Commission, the application of waiting list management can be seen in various scenarios. Here are a few examples:

1. Digital Identity Verification: When individuals apply for a digital identity to access various public services, a waiting list management system can be implemented to handle the influx of applications. This system can prioritize and manage the waiting list based on factors such as urgency, authentication level, or specific service requirements.

2. Access to Digital Services: In cases where certain digital services have limited capacity or resources, a waiting list management system can be utilized to ensure fair access. For instance, if there is a high demand for a specific service, users can be placed on a waiting list and granted access based on availability or priority criteria.

3. Software Updates and Bug Fixes: In the context of IT interoperability, waiting list management can be applied to software updates and bug fixes. When multiple stakeholders are involved in the development and deployment of a digital service, a waiting list can be used to manage the order in which updates or fixes are implemented, ensuring a systematic and coordinated approach.

4. Technical Support and Assistance: For digital public services, a waiting list management system can be employed to handle user requests for technical support or assistance. This system can prioritize and assign support tickets based on urgency, complexity, or predefined criteria, ensuring efficient and timely resolution of issues.

5. Resource Allocation: In situations where there is a limited availability of resources, such as server capacity or storage, a waiting list management system can be utilized to allocate resources fairly. This system can prioritize requests based on factors like urgency, service level agreements, or predefined rules, ensuring optimal utilization of resources.

Overall, waiting list management within the context of IT interoperability in a digital public service allows for efficient handling of user requests, fair resource allocation, and coordinated implementation of updates and fixes.

LOST view: OV-Functional Architecture Principles

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/WaitingListManagementBusinessService

