nce with different legal frameworks and reducing the need for intermediaries in cross-border transactions.&#xA;&#xA;Overall, the application of Blockchain IoP Agreement in European IT interoperability can lead to increased efficiency, transparency, and trust in various sectors, enabling seamless collaboration and data exchange among Member States."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="A Blockchain IoP (Interoperability) Agreement in the context of European IT interoperability among Member States refers to a formal agreement or framework that utilizes blockchain technology to enhance and ensure seamless interoperability between different information systems and digital services across European countries.&#xA;&#xA;Interoperability, in this context, refers to the ability of various IT systems, applications, and services to communicate, exchange data, and work together effectively. It aims to eliminate barriers and enable smooth data sharing, collaboration, and interaction between different systems, regardless of their technological or organizational differences.&#xA;&#xA;The Blockchain IoP Agreement is designed to address the challenges and complexities associated with achieving interoperability in the European Union (EU). It leverages blockchain technology, which is a decentralized and distributed ledger system that enables secure and transparent transactions and data sharing among multiple parties.&#xA;&#xA;The agreement outlines the principles, standards, and protocols that Member States must adhere to in order to achieve interoperability. It establishes a common framework for data exchange, ensuring that information can be seamlessly shared and accessed across different systems while maintaining privacy, security, and data protection.&#xA;&#xA;The blockchain technology employed in the agreement provides several benefits. Firstly, it ensures immutability and tamper-proofing of data, making it highly secure and resistant to unauthorized modifications. Secondly, it enables the creation of smart contracts, which are self-executing agreements that automatically enforce predefined rules and conditions. These smart contracts can facilitate automated and trustless interactions between different systems, reducing the need for intermediaries and enhancing efficiency.&#xA;&#xA;The Blockchain IoP Agreement also defines the governance structure and mechanisms for decision-making, coordination, and dispute resolution among Member States. It establishes a collaborative environment where stakeholders can collectively work towards achieving interoperability goals. This may involve the establishment of a decentralized network or consortium of nodes, where each Member State operates a node and participates in the validation and verification of transactions and data exchanges.&#xA;&#xA;Furthermore, the agreement may address issues related to data standards, semantics, and formats to ensure semantic interoperability, where data can be understood and interpreted consistently across different systems. It may also include provisions for data protection, privacy, and consent management, ensuring compliance with relevant EU regulations such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).&#xA;&#xA;The Blockchain IoP Agreement aims to foster innovation, efficiency, and cost savings by enabling seamless data exchange and collaboration between Member States. It can facilitate the integration of various public services, such as healthcare, transportation, taxation, and e-government, leading to improved citizen experiences and streamlined administrative processes.&#xA;&#xA;Overall, the Blockchain IoP Agreement represents a comprehensive and standardized approach to achieving IT interoperability among European Member States, leveraging blockchain technology to ensure secure, transparent, and efficient data exchange and collaboration."/>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/BlockChainIoPAgreementContract"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:BlockChainIoPAgreementContract"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="A Blockchain IoP Agreement refers to a contractual agreement among European Member states to ensure interoperability in the field of Information Technology (IT). Interoperability, or IoP, refers to the ability of different IT systems and software applications to seamlessly communicate, exchange data, and operate together effectively. The Blockchain IoP Agreement aims to establish a standardized framework using blockchain technology, which enables secure and transparent data sharing and collaboration between various IT systems across European Member states. This agreement ensures that different IT systems can work together efficiently, promoting seamless communication and data exchange, ultimately enhancing the interoperability and efficiency of IT services within the European Union."/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVA-Governance Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TV-Technical Agreements"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVI-Governance Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Technical"/>
      <property key="skos:broader" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/OrganisationalAgreementContract"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Contract" name="Code of Conduct for Energy Efficiency in Data Centres" id="id-521ebc1717934898848ddc1c747c86e9">
      <documentation>Description: This voluntary code of conduct was developed by the European Commission's Joint Research Centre to improve the energy efficiency and environmental sustainability of data centers. It provides guidelines and best practices for data center operators to reduce their environmental impact.

Additional information: The European Code of Conduct for Data Centres is a voluntary initiative managed by the Joint Research Centre (JRC), the European Commission’s science and knowledge service. It primarily addresses data centre owners and operators, and secondly the supply chain and service providers. The Code of Conduct aims to reduce energy consumption in data centres in a cost-effective manner without hampering their critical function.

Example: A data centre operator could conduct an energy audit to identify areas where energy is being wasted. For example, they might find that their servers are using more power than necessary during off-peak hours. Based on these findings, they could create an Action Plan to reduce energy usage, such as implementing server virtualization to consolidate workloads and reduce the number of physical servers needed, or using energy-efficient cooling systems.

Publisher: Joint Research Centre

Source: https://joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu/scientific-activities-z/energy-efficiency/energy-efficiency-products/code-conduct-ict/code-conduct-energy-efficiency-data-centres_en

LOST view: OV-Data Spaces

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/CodeOfConductForEnergyEfficiencyInDataCentresContract

EIRA traceability: eira:OrganisationalAgreementContract

EIRA concept: eira:SolutionBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-07

dct:identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/CodeOfConductForEnergyEfficiencyInDataCentresContract

dct:title: Code of Conduct for Energy Efficiency in Data Centres

</documentation>
