    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Red Hat OpenShift" id="id-d5f38ad4ba334459b6d5533487b7d3a0">
      <documentation>Description: Red Hat OpenShift is a container application platform that enables developers to build, deploy, and manage applications across various cloud environments. It provides a consistent and secure foundation for developing and scaling applications, allowing teams to focus on delivering value rather than managing infrastructure. OpenShift utilizes Kubernetes orchestration to automate the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications.

Additional information: Red Hat OpenShift simplifies the process of developing, deploying, and scaling applications by providing a comprehensive platform that abstracts away the underlying infrastructure complexities. It offers a self-service environment for developers to easily create and deploy applications using containers, which encapsulate the application and its dependencies. OpenShift provides features like automated scaling, load balancing, and continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipelines, enabling teams to rapidly iterate and deliver software. It also offers built-in security features, such as role-based access control (RBAC) and image scanning, to ensure the integrity and compliance of applications. With its support for multiple cloud providers and on-premises environments, OpenShift offers flexibility and portability for applications across different infrastructures.

Example: An example of using Red Hat OpenShift is a development team building a microservices-based application. They can leverage OpenShift to create and manage containers for each microservice, allowing independent development and scaling of individual components. OpenShift's built-in CI/CD capabilities enable automated testing and deployment of new versions, ensuring a streamlined development process. The team can easily scale the application horizontally by adding more container instances as needed, and OpenShift's load balancing features distribute the traffic efficiently. With OpenShift's portability, the team can seamlessly deploy the application across different cloud providers or on-premises environments, adapting to changing business needs.

Publisher: Red Hat

Source: https://www.redhat.com/en/technologies/cloud-computing/openshift

LOST view: TVA-Containerization Enablers [Motivation]

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/RedHatOpenShiftApplicationService

EIRA traceability: eira:DigitalSolutionApplicationService

EIRA concept: eira:SolutionBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-20

dct:identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/RedHatOpenShiftApplicationService

dct:title: Red Hat OpenShift Application Service

</documentation>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/RedHatOpenShiftApplicationService"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2024-01-28"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/RedHatOpenShiftApplicationService"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Red Hat OpenShift Application Service"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="An example of using Red Hat OpenShift is a development team building a microservices-based application. They can leverage OpenShift to create and manage containers for each microservice, allowing independent development and scaling of individual components. OpenShift's built-in CI/CD capabilities enable automated testing and deployment of new versions, ensuring a streamlined development process. The team can easily scale the application horizontally by adding more container instances as needed, and OpenShift's load balancing features distribute the traffic efficiently. With OpenShift's portability, the team can seamlessly deploy the application across different cloud providers or on-premises environments, adapting to changing business needs."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="Red Hat OpenShift simplifies the process of developing, deploying, and scaling applications by providing a comprehensive platform that abstracts away the underlying infrastructure complexities. It offers a self-service environment for developers to easily create and deploy applications using containers, which encapsulate the application and its dependencies. OpenShift provides features like automated scaling, load balancing, and continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipelines, enabling teams to rapidly iterate and deliver software. It also offers built-in security features, such as role-based access control (RBAC) and image scanning, to ensure the integrity and compliance of applications. With its support for multiple cloud providers and on-premises environments, OpenShift offers flexibility and portability for applications across different infrastructures."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:SolutionBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="dct:description" value="Red Hat OpenShift is a container application platform that enables developers to build, deploy, and manage applications across various cloud environments. It provides a consistent and secure foundation for developing and scaling applications, allowing teams to focus on delivering value rather than managing infrastructure. OpenShift utilizes Kubernetes orchestration to automate the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications."/>
      <property key="dct:publisher" value="Red Hat"/>
      <property key="dct:source" value="https://www.redhat.com/en/technologies/cloud-computing/openshift"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVA-Containerization Enablers [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="TechnicalApplication"/>
      <property key="eira:implementedBy" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/DigitalSolutionApplicationService"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Conformance Testing" id="id-da84ac1927bb4d1997a012f3fbd75829">
      <documentation>Definition: Conformance Testing ABB is an Application Service that enables the activities and rules to determine whether a tool, platform or service faithfully meets the requirements of a standard or specification

Source: NIST

Source reference: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ssd/information-systems-group/conformance-testing

Additional information: The logistics of interoperability testing is usually more costly (time, coordination, interoperability), and interoperability testing is no substitute for a conformance test suite. Experience shows that interoperability testing is more successful and less costly when conformance of implementations has been tested first.

Example: The following implementation is an example on how this specific Architecture Building Block (ABB) can be instantiated as a Solution Building Block (SBB): Interoperability Testbed - The Test Bed allows users to execute predefined test cases on their systems in order to test the conformance to standards and requirements, simulate a web-service, validate content and test a complete message exchange protocol. The Testbed manages test cases and users, and orchestrates domain-specific components, the domain-specific logic is in separate components that are used via web services. Test results are provided in a standardised, machine-readable format. The Test Bed also offers a test registry and repository (TRR) to store test artefacts (assertions, test cases, validation schemas, etc.) and compile test services (validation services, simulator services, etc.). Please visit the following website for more information: https://ec.europa.eu/isa2/solutions/interoperability-test-bed_en

IoP Dimension: Behavioral IoP

LOST view: Technical view - Infrastructure

Interoperability saliency: The Conformance Testing Services ABB  is salient for technical interoperability because it enables the validation and verification that several solutions at one or more layers of the interoperability stack and they satisfy certain interoperability requirements.

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/ConformanceTestingApplicationService

eira:ID: ABB128

ABB name: eira:ConformanceTestingApplicationService

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2022-18-01

</documentation>
